s accessible anytime, anywhere. Enhanced security protocols, such as biometric authentication, have also increased user trust.\n\nChanging Consumer Expectations\nModern consumers demand convenience, speed, and transparency. Digital banks offer seamless user experiences, intuitive interfaces, and real-time notifications, aligning with these expectations.\n\nCost Efficiency\nDigital-only banks operate without physical branches, reducing overhead costs. These savings are often passed on to customers through lower fees and higher interest rates on deposits.\n\nBenefits for Personal Finance\n24/7 Access and Control\nUsers can check balances, transfer funds, and pay bills instantly, empowering them to manage their finances proactively.\n\nPersonalized Insights\nMany digital banks use AI to analyze spending patterns and provide tailored budgeting tips, helping users save more effectively.\n\nEnhanced Security\nFeatures like two-factor authentication, instant card freezing, and transaction alerts improve security and reduce fraud risk.\n\nChallenges and Considerations\nCybersecurity Risks\nAs digital banking grows, so do threats from hackers and scammers. Continuous investment in cybersecurity is essential.\n\nDigital Divide\nNot all populations have equal access to digital tools, potentially excluding vulnerable groups from the benefits of digital banking.\n\nRegulatory Compliance\nDigital banks must navigate complex regulations across different jurisdictions, balancing innovation with compliance.\n\nImpact on Financial Habits\nDigital banking encourages financial literacy by providing users with real-time data and educational resources. Automated savings features and spending categorizations help users set and achieve financial goals, fostering healthier financial behaviors.\n\nConclusion\nDigital banking is reshaping personal finance by making money management more accessible, efficient, and secure. While challenges remain, its continued evolution promises to further empower individuals in their financial journeys.'}], 'rag_accuracy': 1.0, 'rag_latency': 0.0039897579699754715}, {'seq_length': 42, 'latency': 1.5987354069948196, 'messages': [{'content': 'What is the title of the biblical scene Leonardo reimagined with dramatic perspective?', 'role': 'user'}, {'content': 'What did Leonardo study to achieve unprecedented realism in his art?', 'role': 'user'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'User prompt: What is the main subject of the Mona Lisa?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: One-Page Report on Art: The Genius of Leonardo da Vinci\nIntroduction\nArt is a profound expression of human creativity, emotion, and intellect. Throughout history, artists have shaped our understanding of the world, challenging conventions and inspiring generations. Among the pantheon of great artists, Leonardo da Vinci stands out as a true polymath whose influence extends far beyond the canvas.\n\nLeonardo da Vinci: The Quintessential Renaissance Man\nLeonardo da Vinci (1452–1519) was an Italian painter, sculptor, inventor, scientist, and engineer. His insatiable curiosity and inventive mind made him the embodiment of the Renaissance humanist ideal. Da Vinci’s art is renowned for its technical mastery, innovative composition, and deep humanity.\n\nArtistic Achievements\nThe Mona Lisa\nPerhaps the most famous painting in the world, the Mona Lisa captivates viewers with her enigmatic smile and lifelike presence. Leonardo’s mastery of sfumato—a technique of subtle gradation between colors and tones—creates a sense of depth and realism that was revolutionary for its time.\n\nThe Last Supper\nIn The Last Supper, Leonardo reimagined a biblical scene with dramatic perspective and psychological intensity. The painting’s composition draws the viewer’s eye to Christ at the center, while the apostles’ varied reactions convey a powerful narrative.\n\nScientific Approach\nLeonardo’s notebooks reveal a relentless quest to understand anatomy, light, and perspective. He dissected human bodies to study muscles and bones, applying this knowledge to achieve unprecedented realism in his art. His sketches, such as the Vitruvian Man, illustrate the harmony between art and science.\n\nLegacy\nLeonardo da Vinci’s influence is immeasurable. He bridged the gap between art and science, inspiring artists, engineers, and thinkers for centuries. His works continue to attract millions of admirers and provoke new interpretations.\n\nConclusion\nLeonardo da Vinci exemplifies the boundless potential of human creativity. His art, driven by curiosity and observation, remains a testament to the enduring power of imagination and intellect. For anyone passionate about the intersection of art, science, and innovation, Leonardo da Vinci is an artist whose legacy is as relevant today as it was five centuries ago.'}], 'rag_accuracy': 1.0, 'rag_latency': 0.003921592608094215}, {'seq_length': 59, 'latency': 0.9229033198207617, 'messages': [{'content': 'What is the title of the biblical scene Leonardo reimagined with dramatic perspective?', 'role': 'user'}, {'content': 'What did Leonardo study to achieve unprecedented realism in his art?', 'role': 'user'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'User prompt: What is the main subject of the Mona Lisa?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: One-Page Report on Art: The Genius of Leonardo da Vinci\nIntroduction\nArt is a profound expression of human creativity, emotion, and intellect. Throughout history, artists have shaped our understanding of the world, challenging conventions and inspiring generations. Among the pantheon of great artists, Leonardo da Vinci stands out as a true polymath whose influence extends far beyond the canvas.\n\nLeonardo da Vinci: The Quintessential Renaissance Man\nLeonardo da Vinci (1452–1519) was an Italian painter, sculptor, inventor, scientist, and engineer. His insatiable curiosity and inventive mind made him the embodiment of the Renaissance humanist ideal. Da Vinci’s art is renowned for its technical mastery, innovative composition, and deep humanity.\n\nArtistic Achievements\nThe Mona Lisa\nPerhaps the most famous painting in the world, the Mona Lisa captivates viewers with her enigmatic smile and lifelike presence. Leonardo’s mastery of sfumato—a technique of subtle gradation between colors and tones—creates a sense of depth and realism that was revolutionary for its time.\n\nThe Last Supper\nIn The Last Supper, Leonardo reimagined a biblical scene with dramatic perspective and psychological intensity. The painting’s composition draws the viewer’s eye to Christ at the center, while the apostles’ varied reactions convey a powerful narrative.\n\nScientific Approach\nLeonardo’s notebooks reveal a relentless quest to understand anatomy, light, and perspective. He dissected human bodies to study muscles and bones, applying this knowledge to achieve unprecedented realism in his art. His sketches, such as the Vitruvian Man, illustrate the harmony between art and science.\n\nLegacy\nLeonardo da Vinci’s influence is immeasurable. He bridged the gap between art and science, inspiring artists, engineers, and thinkers for centuries. His works continue to attract millions of admirers and provoke new interpretations.\n\nConclusion\nLeonardo da Vinci exemplifies the boundless potential of human creativity. His art, driven by curiosity and observation, remains a testament to the enduring power of imagination and intellect. For anyone passionate about the intersection of art, science, and innovation, Leonardo da Vinci is an artist whose legacy is as relevant today as it was five centuries ago.'}], 'rag_accuracy': 1.0, 'rag_latency': 0.003923134878277779}, {'seq_length': 74, 'latency': 0.3562950259074569, 'messages': [{'content': 'What is the title of the biblical scene Leonardo reimagined with dramatic perspective?', 'role': 'user'}, {'content': 'What did Leonardo study to achieve unprecedented realism in his art?', 'role': 'user'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'User prompt: What is the main subject of the Mona Lisa?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: One-Page Report on Art: The Genius of Leonardo da Vinci\nIntroduction\nArt is a profound expression of human creativity, emotion, and intellect. Throughout history, artists have shaped our understanding of the world, challenging conventions and inspiring generations. Among the pantheon of great artists, Leonardo da Vinci stands out as a true polymath whose influence extends far beyond the canvas.\n\nLeonardo da Vinci: The Quintessential Renaissance Man\nLeonardo da Vinci (1452–1519) was an Italian painter, sculptor, inventor, scientist, and engineer. His insatiable curiosity and inventive mind made him the embodiment of the Renaissance humanist ideal. Da Vinci’s art is renowned for its technical mastery, innovative composition, and deep humanity.\n\nArtistic Achievements\nThe Mona Lisa\nPerhaps the most famous painting in the world, the Mona Lisa captivates viewers with her enigmatic smile and lifelike presence. Leonardo’s mastery of sfumato—a technique of subtle gradation between colors and tones—creates a sense of depth and realism that was revolutionary for its time.\n\nThe Last Supper\nIn The Last Supper, Leonardo reimagined a biblical scene with dramatic perspective and psychological intensity. The painting’s composition draws the viewer’s eye to Christ at the center, while the apostles’ varied reactions convey a powerful narrative.\n\nScientific Approach\nLeonardo’s notebooks reveal a relentless quest to understand anatomy, light, and perspective. He dissected human bodies to study muscles and bones, applying this knowledge to achieve unprecedented realism in his art. His sketches, such as the Vitruvian Man, illustrate the harmony between art and science.\n\nLegacy\nLeonardo da Vinci’s influence is immeasurable. He bridged the gap between art and science, inspiring artists, engineers, and thinkers for centuries. His works continue to attract millions of admirers and provoke new interpretations.\n\nConclusion\nLeonardo da Vinci exemplifies the boundless potential of human creativity. His art, driven by curiosity and observation, remains a testament to the enduring power of imagination and intellect. For anyone passionate about the intersection of art, science, and innovation, Leonardo da Vinci is an artist whose legacy is as relevant today as it was five centuries ago.'}], 'rag_accuracy': 1.0, 'rag_latency': 0.003850809298455715}, {'seq_length': 44, 'latency': 0.44692283496260643, 'messages': [{'content': 'What is the title of the book by Daniel J. Levitin referenced in the report?', 'role': 'user'}, {'content': 'Which brain imaging technique is cited as useful for studying music’s effect on the brain?', 'role': 'user'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'User prompt: Which brain region processes sound when listening to music?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: The Science of Music and Its Impact on the Brain\nIntroduction\nMusic is a universal language, transcending cultural and linguistic barriers. It has been an integral part of human civilization, serving as a medium for expression, communication, and social bonding. In recent years, the scientific community has made significant strides in understanding how music affects the brain, cognition, and emotional well-being.\n\nThe Neuroscience of Music\nWhen we listen to or perform music, multiple regions of the brain are activated. The auditory cortex processes sound, while the motor cortex is involved when playing an instrument or even tapping along to a beat. The limbic system, which governs emotions, is also deeply engaged, explaining why music can evoke powerful feelings.\n\nFunctional MRI (fMRI) studies have shown that music stimulates the release of dopamine—the "feel-good" neurotransmitter—similar to other pleasurable activities like eating or socializing. This neural reward system activation helps explain music\'s mood-enhancing effects.\n\nCognitive and Emotional Benefits\nMemory and Learning\nMusic has been linked to improved memory and learning. The "Mozart effect," though debated, suggests that listening to certain types of music may temporarily enhance spatial-temporal reasoning. More robust evidence supports the use of music therapy for patients with Alzheimer\'s disease and other forms of dementia, where familiar melodies can trigger memories and improve cognitive function.\n\nEmotional Regulation\nMusic is a powerful tool for emotional regulation. It can reduce stress, alleviate anxiety, and even help manage pain. Studies show that listening to calming music lowers cortisol levels and slows heart rate, contributing to relaxation and well-being.\n\nMusic and Social Connection\nEngaging with music—whether through group singing, playing in a band, or dancing—fosters social bonds. These shared musical experiences can enhance empathy, cooperation, and a sense of community. This is particularly evident in cultural rituals, celebrations, and even protest movements, where music unites individuals around a common cause.\n\nConclusion\nMusic is more than just an art form; it is a complex, multisensory experience that shapes our brains, emotions, and social lives. As research continues to unravel the intricate relationship between music and the mind, it becomes increasingly clear that music is a fundamental part of what makes us human.'}], 'rag_accuracy': 1.0, 'rag_latency': 0.015777772292494774}, {'seq_length': 66, 'latency': 0.29235036578029394, 'messages': [{'content': 'What is the title of the book by Daniel J. Levitin referenced in the report?', 'role': 'user'}, {'content': 'Which brain imaging technique is cited as useful for studying music’s effect on the brain?', 'role': 'user'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'User prompt: Which brain region processes sound when listening to music?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: The Science of Music and Its Impact on the Brain\nIntroduction\nMusic is a universal language, transcending cultural and linguistic barriers. It has been an integral part of human civilization, serving as a medium for expression, communication, and social bonding. In recent years, the scientific community has made significant strides in understanding how music affects the brain, cognition, and emotional well-being.\n\nThe Neuroscience of Music\nWhen we listen to or perform music, multiple regions of the brain are activated. The auditory cortex processes sound, while the motor cortex is involved when playing an instrument or even tapping along to a beat. The limbic system, which governs emotions, is also deeply engaged, explaining why music can evoke powerful feelings.\n\nFunctional MRI (fMRI) studies have shown that music stimulates the release of dopamine—the "feel-good" neurotransmitter—similar to other pleasurable activities like eating or socializing. This neural reward system activation helps explain music\'s mood-enhancing effects.\n\nCognitive and Emotional Benefits\nMemory and Learning\nMusic has been linked to improved memory and learning. The "Mozart effect," though debated, suggests that listening to certain types of music may temporarily enhance spatial-temporal reasoning. More robust evidence supports the use of music therapy for patients with Alzheimer\'s disease and other forms of dementia, where familiar melodies can trigger memories and improve cognitive function.\n\nEmotional Regulation\nMusic is a powerful tool for emotional regulation. It can reduce stress, alleviate anxiety, and even help manage pain. Studies show that listening to calming music lowers cortisol levels and slows heart rate, contributing to relaxation and well-being.\n\nMusic and Social Connection\nEngaging with music—whether through group singing, playing in a band, or dancing—fosters social bonds. These shared musical experiences can enhance empathy, cooperation, and a sense of community. This is particularly evident in cultural rituals, celebrations, and even protest movements, where music unites individuals around a common cause.\n\nConclusion\nMusic is more than just an art form; it is a complex, multisensory experience that shapes our brains, emotions, and social lives. As research continues to unravel the intricate relationship between music and the mind, it becomes increasingly clear that music is a fundamental part of what makes us human.'}], 'rag_accuracy': 1.0, 'rag_latency': 0.003876551054418087}, {'seq_length': 81, 'latency': 0.2924875561147928, 'messages': [{'content': 'What is the title of the book by Daniel J. Levitin referenced in the report?', 'role': 'user'}, {'content': 'Which brain imaging technique is cited as useful for studying music’s effect on the brain?', 'role': 'user'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'User prompt: Which brain region processes sound when listening to music?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: The Science of Music and Its Impact on the Brain\nIntroduction\nMusic is a universal language, transcending cultural and linguistic barriers. It has been an integral part of human civilization, serving as a medium for expression, communication, and social bonding. In recent years, the scientific community has made significant strides in understanding how music affects the brain, cognition, and emotional well-being.\n\nThe Neuroscience of Music\nWhen we listen to or perform music, multiple regions of the brain are activated. The auditory cortex processes sound, while the motor cortex is involved when playing an instrument or even tapping along to a beat. The limbic system, which governs emotions, is also deeply engaged, explaining why music can evoke powerful feelings.\n\nFunctional MRI (fMRI) studies have shown that music stimulates the release of dopamine—the "feel-good" neurotransmitter—similar to other pleasurable activities like eating or socializing. This neural reward system activation helps explain music\'s mood-enhancing effects.\n\nCognitive and Emotional Benefits\nMemory and Learning\nMusic has been linked to improved memory and learning. The "Mozart effect," though debated, suggests that listening to certain types of music may temporarily enhance spatial-temporal reasoning. More robust evidence supports the use of music therapy for patients with Alzheimer\'s disease and other forms of dementia, where familiar melodies can trigger memories and improve cognitive function.\n\nEmotional Regulation\nMusic is a powerful tool for emotional regulation. It can reduce stress, alleviate anxiety, and even help manage pain. Studies show that listening to calming music lowers cortisol levels and slows heart rate, contributing to relaxation and well-being.\n\nMusic and Social Connection\nEngaging with music—whether through group singing, playing in a band, or dancing—fosters social bonds. These shared musical experiences can enhance empathy, cooperation, and a sense of community. This is particularly evident in cultural rituals, celebrations, and even protest movements, where music unites individuals around a common cause.\n\nConclusion\nMusic is more than just an art form; it is a complex, multisensory experience that shapes our brains, emotions, and social lives. As research continues to unravel the intricate relationship between music and the mind, it becomes increasingly clear that music is a fundamental part of what makes us human.'}], 'rag_accuracy': 1.0, 'rag_latency': 0.003865336999297142}, {'seq_length': 41, 'latency': 0.910356649197638, 'messages': [{'content': 'Which prior systems does Metron build upon according to the related work section?', 'role': 'user'}, {'content': 'What challenge in NFV does Metron address according to the introduction?', 'role': 'user'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'User prompt: What hardware resources does Metron utilize to run stateful service chains?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: Metron: NFV Service Chains at the True Speed of the Underlying Hardware  \nAuthors: Georgios P. Katsikas, Tom Barbette, Dejan Kostić, Rebecca Steinert, Gerald Q. Maguire Jr.\n\nAbstract  \nMetron is an NFV platform that fully utilizes commodity server and network resources to run stateful service chains efficiently. It achieves zero inter-core communication using smart tagging and hardware dispatching, attaining up to 100 Gbps performance on a single server.\n\n1. Introduction  \nNFV seeks to replace hardware middleboxes with software NFs. While beneficial, it faces performance challenges. Metron eliminates packet handovers between cores using hardware tagging and dispatching and achieves superior throughput and lower latency than OpenBox and E2.\n\n2. System Architecture  \n2.1 Overview  \nMetron identifies traffic classes, synthesizes processing graphs, and uses programmable switches and NICs to implement stateless tasks. The rest is executed on designated CPU cores, using NIC-based tags for traffic steering.\n\n2.2 Data Plane  \nA master-slave architecture is adopted, where the master agent manages hardware bindings and delegates processing tasks to slaves. NICs dispatch packets directly to appropriate CPU cores, improving speed.\n\n2.3 Control Plane  \nGraph synthesis via SNF merges NF logic, while placement and dynamic scaling strategies minimize overhead. Traffic classes are used to scale NFs efficiently without duplicating logic unnecessarily.\n\n2.4 Routing and Failures  \nMetron installs tagged OpenFlow rules for routing and dispatching. Backup rules support fast failover, and controller updates switches and NICs during path changes.\n\n3. Evaluation  \n3.1 Large-Scale Deployment  \nMetron performs DPI at 40 Gbps and supports stateful service chains at 100 Gbps. It outperforms OpenBox and E2 by a large margin in both latency and throughput, using fewer CPU cores.\n\n3.2 Dynamic Scaling  \nMetron adapts to workload changes by tagging traffic classes and redirecting them across CPU cores, offering fast and fine-grained scaling with minimal latency.\n\n3.3 Deployment Micro-benchmarks  \nMetron installs thousands of rules within hundreds of milliseconds, using hardware/software combination. It handles diverse switch capabilities and supports deployments across large-scale topologies efficiently.\n\n4. Related Work  \nMetron builds upon ideas from E2, OpenBox, SNF, and modern NIC capabilities. Unlike others, it aggressively uses hardware offloading and intelligent placement for performance.\n\n5. Conclusion  \nMetron demonstrates that efficient NFV service chains can be realized on commodity servers by leveraging hardware-aware placement, dispatching, and offloading strategies. It significantly outperforms state-of-the-art systems in both throughput and latency.\n\n6. Acknowledgments  \nThanks to funding from the Swedish Foundation for Strategic Research and WASP.'}], 'rag_accuracy': 1.0, 'rag_latency': 0.008030983619391918}, {'seq_length': 60, 'latency': 0.49167751520872116, 'messages': [{'content': 'Which prior systems does Metron build upon according to the related work section?', 'role': 'user'}, {'content': 'What challenge in NFV does Metron address according to the introduction?', 'role': 'user'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'User prompt: What hardware resources does Metron utilize to run stateful service chains?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: Metron: NFV Service Chains at the True Speed of the Underlying Hardware  \nAuthors: Georgios P. Katsikas, Tom Barbette, Dejan Kostić, Rebecca Steinert, Gerald Q. Maguire Jr.\n\nAbstract  \nMetron is an NFV platform that fully utilizes commodity server and network resources to run stateful service chains efficiently. It achieves zero inter-core communication using smart tagging and hardware dispatching, attaining up to 100 Gbps performance on a single server.\n\n1. Introduction  \nNFV seeks to replace hardware middleboxes with software NFs. While beneficial, it faces performance challenges. Metron eliminates packet handovers between cores using hardware tagging and disp