re placement, dispatching, and offloading strategies. It significantly outperforms state-of-the-art systems in both throughput and latency.\n\n6. Acknowledgments  \nThanks to funding from the Swedish Foundation for Strategic Research and WASP.'}], 'rag_accuracy': 1.0, 'rag_latency': 0.00395286176353693}, {'seq_length': 79, 'latency': 0.25706504471600056, 'messages': [{'content': 'Which prior systems does Metron build upon according to the related work section?', 'role': 'user'}, {'content': 'What challenge in NFV does Metron address according to the introduction?', 'role': 'user'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'User prompt: What hardware resources does Metron utilize to run stateful service chains?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: Metron: NFV Service Chains at the True Speed of the Underlying Hardware  \nAuthors: Georgios P. Katsikas, Tom Barbette, Dejan Kostić, Rebecca Steinert, Gerald Q. Maguire Jr.\n\nAbstract  \nMetron is an NFV platform that fully utilizes commodity server and network resources to run stateful service chains efficiently. It achieves zero inter-core communication using smart tagging and hardware dispatching, attaining up to 100 Gbps performance on a single server.\n\n1. Introduction  \nNFV seeks to replace hardware middleboxes with software NFs. While beneficial, it faces performance challenges. Metron eliminates packet handovers between cores using hardware tagging and dispatching and achieves superior throughput and lower latency than OpenBox and E2.\n\n2. System Architecture  \n2.1 Overview  \nMetron identifies traffic classes, synthesizes processing graphs, and uses programmable switches and NICs to implement stateless tasks. The rest is executed on designated CPU cores, using NIC-based tags for traffic steering.\n\n2.2 Data Plane  \nA master-slave architecture is adopted, where the master agent manages hardware bindings and delegates processing tasks to slaves. NICs dispatch packets directly to appropriate CPU cores, improving speed.\n\n2.3 Control Plane  \nGraph synthesis via SNF merges NF logic, while placement and dynamic scaling strategies minimize overhead. Traffic classes are used to scale NFs efficiently without duplicating logic unnecessarily.\n\n2.4 Routing and Failures  \nMetron installs tagged OpenFlow rules for routing and dispatching. Backup rules support fast failover, and controller updates switches and NICs during path changes.\n\n3. Evaluation  \n3.1 Large-Scale Deployment  \nMetron performs DPI at 40 Gbps and supports stateful service chains at 100 Gbps. It outperforms OpenBox and E2 by a large margin in both latency and throughput, using fewer CPU cores.\n\n3.2 Dynamic Scaling  \nMetron adapts to workload changes by tagging traffic classes and redirecting them across CPU cores, offering fast and fine-grained scaling with minimal latency.\n\n3.3 Deployment Micro-benchmarks  \nMetron installs thousands of rules within hundreds of milliseconds, using hardware/software combination. It handles diverse switch capabilities and supports deployments across large-scale topologies efficiently.\n\n4. Related Work  \nMetron builds upon ideas from E2, OpenBox, SNF, and modern NIC capabilities. Unlike others, it aggressively uses hardware offloading and intelligent placement for performance.\n\n5. Conclusion  \nMetron demonstrates that efficient NFV service chains can be realized on commodity servers by leveraging hardware-aware placement, dispatching, and offloading strategies. It significantly outperforms state-of-the-art systems in both throughput and latency.\n\n6. Acknowledgments  \nThanks to funding from the Swedish Foundation for Strategic Research and WASP.'}], 'rag_accuracy': 1.0, 'rag_latency': 0.003946427255868912}, {'seq_length': 38, 'latency': 2.7314965268597007, 'messages': [{'content': 'How does CHEETAH maintain backward compatibility with existing infrastructure?', 'role': 'user'}, {'content': 'Which TCP field is repurposed for cookie embedding?', 'role': 'user'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "User prompt: What critical limitation do traditional stateless load balancers face regarding server changes?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: A High-Speed Load-Balancer Design with Guaranteed Per-Connection-Consistency\nAuthors: Tom Barbette, Chen Tang, Haoran Yao, Dejan Kostić, Gerald Q. Maguire Jr., Panagiotis Papadimitratos, Marco Chiesa\n\nAbstract\nCHEETAH is a load balancer designed to achieve uniform load distribution and per-connection-consistency (PCC), two goals that are traditionally hard to achieve simultaneously. It encodes server identifiers in a cookie within packets to ensure PCC under dynamic changes in server/load balancer pools. CHEETAH works in both stateless and stateful modes and performs comparably or better than existing systems.\n\n1 Introduction\nDatacenters face high demand for load balancers, but current solutions often trade off PCC for uniform load distribution. This paper introduces CHEETAH, a novel system that guarantees PCC and uniform distribution without such trade-offs, using packet-embedded cookies.\n\n2 Background and Motivation\nExisting stateless and stateful LBs struggle with PCC and load distribution. Stateless LBs are simple but can't handle server changes without breaking connections. Stateful LBs can preserve PCC but suffer from slow hash table operations and DDoS vulnerabilities.\n\n3 The CHEETAH Load Balancer\nCHEETAH stores mapping information inside the packets, enabling any LB mechanism to operate without violating PCC. Stateless CHEETAH uses cookies derived via hashing and XORing, while stateful CHEETAH uses cookies to index into simplified stack-based state tables, increasing performance and resilience.\n\n4 Implementation\nImplemented in FastClick and P4-Tofino, CHEETAH integrates with TCP timestamp fields for cookie embedding. The system supports multiple LB strategies like round-robin, power-of-two choices, and weighted round-robin, and is backward-compatible with existing infrastructure.\n\n5 Evaluation\nExperiments demonstrate CHEETAH’s high efficiency. Stateless CHEETAH offers minimal overhead and achieves close to optimal load balancing with advanced strategies. It reduces flow completion times (FCT) by up to 3x and avoids PCC violations even during server pool changes.\n\n6 Frequently Asked Questions\nCHEETAH maintains resilience levels of existing systems, hides server count using hashed cookies, and supports multipath protocols by extending the cookie scheme.\n\n7 Related Work\nPrior work includes hash-based and stateful LBs like Beamer, Ananta, and Maglev. CHEETAH differentiates by decoupling PCC from LB logic and optimizing state handling.\n\n8 Conclusions\nCHEETAH provides a general framework for efficient and resilient LBs that support advanced mechanisms while preserving PCC. Future work includes new mechanisms and improved middlebox compatibility.\n\nAppendices\nA: Theoretical proof shows cookies must scale logarithmically with the number of servers.  \nB: Strategies to minimize cookie size.  \nC: Details on TCP timestamp encoding and deployment compatibility.  \nD: Empirical timestamp granularity measurements confirm CHEETAH compatibility."}], 'rag_accuracy': 1.0, 'rag_latency': 0.012575116939842701}, {'seq_length': 54, 'latency': 2.137707524932921, 'messages': [{'content': 'How does CHEETAH maintain backward compatibility with existing infrastructure?', 'role': 'user'}, {'content': 'Which TCP field is repurposed for cookie embedding?', 'role': 'user'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "User prompt: What critical limitation do traditional stateless load balancers face regarding server changes?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: A High-Speed Load-Balancer Design with Guaranteed Per-Connection-Consistency\nAuthors: Tom Barbette, Chen Tang, Haoran Yao, Dejan Kostić, Gerald Q. Maguire Jr., Panagiotis Papadimitratos, Marco Chiesa\n\nAbstract\nCHEETAH is a load balancer designed to achieve uniform load distribution and per-connection-consistency (PCC), two goals that are traditionally hard to achieve simultaneously. It encodes server identifiers in a cookie within packets to ensure PCC under dynamic changes in server/load balancer pools. CHEETAH works in both stateless and stateful modes and performs comparably or better than existing systems.\n\n1 Introduction\nDatacenters face high demand for load balancers, but current solutions often trade off PCC for uniform load distribution. This paper introduces CHEETAH, a novel system that guarantees PCC and uniform distribution without such trade-offs, using packet-embedded cookies.\n\n2 Background and Motivation\nExisting stateless and stateful LBs struggle with PCC and load distribution. Stateless LBs are simple but can't handle server changes without breaking connections. Stateful LBs can preserve PCC but suffer from slow hash table operations and DDoS vulnerabilities.\n\n3 The CHEETAH Load Balancer\nCHEETAH stores mapping information inside the packets, enabling any LB mechanism to operate without violating PCC. Stateless CHEETAH uses cookies derived via hashing and XORing, while stateful CHEETAH uses cookies to index into simplified stack-based state tables, increasing performance and resilience.\n\n4 Implementation\nImplemented in FastClick and P4-Tofino, CHEETAH integrates with TCP timestamp fields for cookie embedding. The system supports multiple LB strategies like round-robin, power-of-two choices, and weighted round-robin, and is backward-compatible with existing infrastructure.\n\n5 Evaluation\nExperiments demonstrate CHEETAH’s high efficiency. Stateless CHEETAH offers minimal overhead and achieves close to optimal load balancing with advanced strategies. It reduces flow completion times (FCT) by up to 3x and avoids PCC violations even during server pool changes.\n\n6 Frequently Asked Questions\nCHEETAH maintains resilience levels of existing systems, hides server count using hashed cookies, and supports multipath protocols by extending the cookie scheme.\n\n7 Related Work\nPrior work includes hash-based and stateful LBs like Beamer, Ananta, and Maglev. CHEETAH differentiates by decoupling PCC from LB logic and optimizing state handling.\n\n8 Conclusions\nCHEETAH provides a general framework for efficient and resilient LBs that support advanced mechanisms while preserving PCC. Future work includes new mechanisms and improved middlebox compatibility.\n\nAppendices\nA: Theoretical proof shows cookies must scale logarithmically with the number of servers.  \nB: Strategies to minimize cookie size.  \nC: Details on TCP timestamp encoding and deployment compatibility.  \nD: Empirical timestamp granularity measurements confirm CHEETAH compatibility."}], 'rag_accuracy': 1.0, 'rag_latency': 0.0042051831260323524}, {'seq_length': 74, 'latency': 1.9945775168016553, 'messages': [{'content': 'How does CHEETAH maintain backward compatibility with existing infrastructure?', 'role': 'user'}, {'content': 'Which TCP field is repurposed for cookie embedding?', 'role': 'user'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "User prompt: What critical limitation do traditional stateless load balancers face regarding server changes?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: A High-Speed Load-Balancer Design with Guaranteed Per-Connection-Consistency\nAuthors: Tom Barbette, Chen Tang, Haoran Yao, Dejan Kostić, Gerald Q. Maguire Jr., Panagiotis Papadimitratos, Marco Chiesa\n\nAbstract\nCHEETAH is a load balancer designed to achieve uniform load distribution and per-connection-consistency (PCC), two goals that are traditionally hard to achieve simultaneously. It encodes server identifiers in a cookie within packets to ensure PCC under dynamic changes in server/load balancer pools. CHEETAH works in both stateless and stateful modes and performs comparably or better than existing systems.\n\n1 Introduction\nDatacenters face high demand for load balancers, but current solutions often trade off PCC for uniform load distribution. This paper introduces CHEETAH, a novel system that guarantees PCC and uniform distribution without such trade-offs, using packet-embedded cookies.\n\n2 Background and Motivation\nExisting stateless and stateful LBs struggle with PCC and load distribution. Stateless LBs are simple but can't handle server changes without breaking connections. Stateful LBs can preserve PCC but suffer from slow hash table operations and DDoS vulnerabilities.\n\n3 The CHEETAH Load Balancer\nCHEETAH stores mapping information inside the packets, enabling any LB mechanism to operate without violating PCC. Stateless CHEETAH uses cookies derived via hashing and XORing, while stateful CHEETAH uses cookies to index into simplified stack-based state tables, increasing performance and resilience.\n\n4 Implementation\nImplemented in FastClick and P4-Tofino, CHEETAH integrates with TCP timestamp fields for cookie embedding. The system supports multiple LB strategies like round-robin, power-of-two choices, and weighted round-robin, and is backward-compatible with existing infrastructure.\n\n5 Evaluation\nExperiments demonstrate CHEETAH’s high efficiency. Stateless CHEETAH offers minimal overhead and achieves close to optimal load balancing with advanced strategies. It reduces flow completion times (FCT) by up to 3x and avoids PCC violations even during server pool changes.\n\n6 Frequently Asked Questions\nCHEETAH maintains resilience levels of existing systems, hides server count using hashed cookies, and supports multipath protocols by extending the cookie scheme.\n\n7 Related Work\nPrior work includes hash-based and stateful LBs like Beamer, Ananta, and Maglev. CHEETAH differentiates by decoupling PCC from LB logic and optimizing state handling.\n\n8 Conclusions\nCHEETAH provides a general framework for efficient and resilient LBs that support advanced mechanisms while preserving PCC. Future work includes new mechanisms and improved middlebox compatibility.\n\nAppendices\nA: Theoretical proof shows cookies must scale logarithmically with the number of servers.  \nB: Strategies to minimize cookie size.  \nC: Details on TCP timestamp encoding and deployment compatibility.  \nD: Empirical timestamp granularity measurements confirm CHEETAH compatibility."}], 'rag_accuracy': 1.0, 'rag_latency': 0.004126108251512051}, {'seq_length': 51, 'latency': 0.5862769307568669, 'messages': [{'content': 'By what percentage does Crux outperform Sincronia, TACCL, and CASSINI in trace simulations?', 'role': 'user'}, {'content': "How many lines of code comprise Crux's implementation?", 'role': 'user'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "User prompt: Which algorithm does Crux use for priority compression?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: Crux: GPU-Efficient Communication Scheduling for Deep Learning Training  \nAuthors: Jiamin Cao, Yu Guan, Kun Qian, Jiaqi Gao, Wencong Xiao, Jianbo Dong, Binzhang Fu, Dennis Cai, Ennan Zhai (Alibaba Cloud)\n\nAbstract  \nCrux is a communication scheduler that addresses inter-job communication contention in deep learning training (DLT) clusters, improving GPU utilization. By defining GPU intensity and tailoring scheduling accordingly, Crux increases GPU utilization up to 23% over state-of-the-art approaches.\n\n1. Introduction  \nModern GPU clusters face GPU underutilization due to communication contention among co-executing DLT jobs. Crux introduces GPU intensity as a prioritization metric and shows that current schedulers don't adequately handle inter-job contention.\n\n2. Background and Motivation  \nProfiling a 2,000+ GPU production cluster shows that 36.3% of jobs face contention. Inter-job communication contention significantly affects GPU utilization. The paper argues that optimizing GPU utilization—not just JCT—is critical for cost and efficiency.\n\n3. Methodology and System Overview  \nMaximizing GPU utilization is modeled as a flow scheduling problem using GPU intensity (computation/communication ratio). The system overview includes GPU intensity-based path selection, priority assignment, and priority compression.\n\n4. Crux Design  \n- 4.1 Path Selection: Avoids congestion for high-intensity jobs.  \n- 4.2 Priority Assignment: Corrects GPU intensity based on job characteristics (e.g., iteration time, overlap).  \n- 4.3 Priority Compression: Uses DAG-based Max-K-Cut to map many jobs to limited NIC priority levels.  \n- 4.4 Validation: Simulations show Crux achieves over 97% optimal performance for each scheduling step.\n\n5. Implementation  \nCrux is implemented in 7K LOC and integrates with PyTorch, TensorFlow, and X-DeepLearning. Crux Daemon and Crux Transport modules handle job profiling, probing network paths, and enforcing decisions via RDMA APIs.\n\n6. Evaluation  \n- 6.1 Testbed: 96-GPU testbed shows up to 14.8% GPU utilization gain and 33% JCT reduction.  \n- 6.2 Trace Simulation: On a 2,000+ GPU cluster trace, Crux outperforms Sincronia, TACCL, and CASSINI by 4–23%.  \n- 6.3 Scheduler Compatibility: Crux boosts performance even when combined with HiveD or Muri.  \n- 6.4 GPU Intensity Visualization: Darker flow patterns correlate with higher GPU utilization under Crux.\n\n7. Discussions  \n- 7.1 Limitations: Crux simplifies complex overlap patterns; real-world variance may cause suboptimal prioritization.  \n- 7.2 Fairness: Higher priority jobs may slightly degrade others; fairness extensions possible.  \n- 7.3 Topology Adaptability: Crux’s design generalizes across Clos, double-sided, and potentially other topologies.\n\n8. Related Work  \nCrux differs from job-level schedulers and prior intra-job communication solutions. It also surpasses inter-job tools like CASSINI by using real-time GPU intensity metrics and adaptive scheduling.\n\n9. Conclusion  \nCrux introduces GPU intensity as a foundational metric for DLT communication scheduling. Through path selection, priority assignment, and priority compression, it significantly improves cluster GPU utilization.\n\nAppendices  \n- A: Proof that GPU intensity-based flow scheduling correlates with utilization.  \n- B: Theoretical grounding for DAG-based Max-K-Cut in priority compression."}], 'rag_accuracy': 1.0, 'rag_latency': 0.0038903644308447838}, {'seq_length': 67, 'latency': 1.0070483312010765, 'messages': [{'content': 'By what percentage does Crux outperform Sincronia, TACCL, and CASSINI in trace simulations?', 'role': 'user'}, {'content': "How many lines of code comprise Crux's implementation?", 'role': 'user'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "User prompt: Which algorithm does Crux use for priority compression?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: Crux: GPU-Efficient Communication Scheduling for Deep Learning Training  \nAuthors: Jiamin Cao, Yu Guan, Kun Qian, Jiaqi Gao, Wencong Xiao, Jianbo Dong, Binzhang Fu, Dennis Cai, Ennan Zhai (Alibaba Cloud)\n\nAbstract  \nCrux is a communication scheduler that addresses inter-job communication contention in deep learning training (DLT) clusters, improving GPU utilization. By defining GPU intensity and tailoring scheduling accordingly, Crux increases GPU utilization up to 23% over state-of-the-art approaches.\n\n1. Introduction  \nModern GPU clusters face GPU underutilization due to communication contention among co-executing DLT jobs. Crux introduces GPU intensity as a prioritization metric and shows that current schedulers don't adequately handle inter-job contention.\n\n2. Background and Motivation  \nProfiling a 2,000+ GPU production cluster shows that 36.3% of jobs face contention. Inter-job communication contention significantly affects GPU utilization. The paper argues that optimizing GPU utilization—not just JCT—is critical for cost and efficiency.\n\n3. Methodology and System Overview  \nMaximizing GPU utilization is modeled as a flow scheduling problem using GPU intensity (computation/communication ratio). The system overview includes GPU intensity-based path selection, priority assignment, and priority compression.\n\n4. Crux Design  \n- 4.1 Path Selection: Avoids congestion for high-intensity jobs.  \n- 4.2 Priority Assignment: Corrects GPU intensity based on job characteristics (e.g., iteration time, overlap).  \n- 4.3 Priority Compression: Uses DAG-based Max-K-Cut to map many jobs to limited NIC priority levels.  \n- 4.4 Validation: Simulations show Crux achieves over 97% optimal performance for each scheduling step.\n\n5. Implementation  \nCrux is implemented in 7K LOC and integrates with PyTorch, TensorFlow, and X-DeepLearning. Crux Daemon and Crux Transport modules handle job profiling, probing network paths, and enforcing decisions via RDMA APIs.\n\n6. Evaluation  \n- 6.1 Testbed: 96-GPU testbed shows up to 14.8% GPU utilization gain and 33% JCT reduction.  \n- 6.2 Trace Simulation: On a 2,000+ GPU cluster trace, Crux outperforms Sincronia, TACCL, and CASSINI by 4–23%.  \n- 6.3 Scheduler Compatibility: Crux boosts performance even when combined with HiveD or Muri.  \n- 6.4 GPU Intensity Visualization: Darker flow patterns correlate with higher GPU utilization under Crux.\n\n7. Discussions  \n- 7.1 Limitations: Crux simplifies complex overlap patterns; real-world variance may cause suboptimal prioritization.  \n- 7.2 Fairness: Higher priority jobs may slightly degrade others; fairness extensions possible.  \n- 7.3 Topology Adaptability: Crux’s design generalizes across Clos, double-sided, and potentially other topologies.\n\n8. Related Work  \nCrux differs from job-level schedulers and prior intra-job communication solutions. It also surpasses inter-job tools like CASSINI by using real-time GPU intensity metrics and adaptive scheduling.\n\n9. Conclusion  \nCrux introduces GPU intensity as a foundational metric for DLT communication scheduling. Through path selection, priority assignment, and priority compression, it significantly improves cluster GPU utilization.\n\nAppendices  \n- A: Proof that GPU intensity-based flow scheduling correlates with utilization.  \n- B: Theoretical grounding for DAG-based Max-K-Cut in priority compression."}], 'rag_accuracy': 1.0, 'rag_latency': 0.0075135137885808945}, {'seq_length': 82, 'latency': 0.08654316887259483, 'messages': [{'content': 'By what percentage does Crux outperform Sincronia, TACCL, and CASSINI in trace simulations?', 'role': 'user'}, {'content': "How many lines of code comprise Crux's implementation?", 'role': 'user'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "User prompt: Which algorithm does Crux use for priority compression?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: Crux: GPU-Efficient Communication Scheduling for Deep Learning Training  \nAuthors: Jiamin Cao, Yu Guan, Kun Qian, Jiaqi Gao, Wencong Xiao, Jianbo Dong, Binzhang Fu, Dennis Cai, Ennan Zhai (Alibaba Cloud)\n\nAbstract  \nCrux is a communication scheduler that addresses inter-job communication contention in deep learning training (DLT) clusters, improving GPU utilization. By defining GPU intensity and tailoring scheduling accordingly, Crux increases GPU utilization up to 23% over state-of-the-art approaches.\n\n1. Introduction  \nModern GPU clusters face GPU underutilization due to communication contention among co-executing DLT jobs. Crux introduces GPU intensity as a prioritization metric and shows that current schedulers don't adequately handle inter-job contention.\n\n2. Background and Motivation  \nProfiling a 2,000+ GPU production cluster shows that 36.3% of jobs face contention. Inter-job communication contention significantly affects GPU utilization. The paper argues that optimizing GPU utilization—not just JCT—is critical for cost and efficiency.\n\n3. Methodology and System Overview  \nMaximizing GPU utilization is modeled as a flow scheduling problem using GPU intensity (computation/communication ratio). The system overview includes GPU intensity-based path selection, priority assignment, and priority compression.\n\n4. Crux Design  \n- 4.1 Path Selection: Avoids congestion for high-intensity jobs.  \n- 4.2 Priority Assignment: Corrects GPU intensity based on job characteristics (e.g., iteration time, overlap).  \n- 4.3 Priority Compression: Uses DAG-based Max-K-Cut to map many jobs to limited NIC priority levels.  \n- 4.4 Validation: Simulations show Crux achieves over 97% optimal performance for each scheduling step.\n\n5. Implementation  \nCrux is implemented in 7K LOC and integrates with PyTorch, TensorFlow, and X-DeepLearning. Crux Daemon and Crux Transport modules handle job profiling, probing network paths, and enforcing decisions via RDMA APIs.\n\n6. Evaluation  \n- 6.1 Testbed: 96-GPU testbed shows up to 14.8% GPU utilization gain and 33% JCT reduction.  \n- 6.2 Trace Simulation: On a 2,000+ GPU cluster trace, Crux outperforms Sincronia, TACCL, and CASSINI by 4–23%.  \n- 6.3 Scheduler Compatibility: Crux boosts performance even when combined with HiveD or Muri.  \n- 6.4 GPU Intensity Visualization: Darker flow patterns correlate with higher GPU utilization under Crux.\n\n7. Discussions  \n- 7.1 Limitations: Crux simplifies complex overlap patterns; real-world variance may cause suboptimal prioritization.  \n- 7.2 Fairness: Higher priority jobs may slightly degrade others; fairness extensions possible.  \n- 7.3 Topology Adaptability: Crux’s design generalizes across Clos, double-sided, and potentially other topologies.\n\n8. Related Work  \nCrux differs from job-level schedulers and prior intra-job communication solutions. It also surpasses inter-job tools like CASSINI by using real-time GPU intensity metrics and adaptive scheduling.\n\n9. Conclusion  \nCrux introduces GPU intensity as a foundational metric for DLT communication scheduling. Through path selection, priority assignment, and priority compression, it significantly improves cluster GPU utilization.\n\nAppendices  \n- A: Proof that GPU intensity-based flow scheduling correlates with utilization.  \n- B: Theoretical grounding for DAG-based Max-K-Cut in priority compression."}], 'rag_accuracy': 1.0, 'rag_latency': 0.0038822852075099945}, {'seq_length': 39, 'latency': 0.5559317348524928, 'messages': [{'content': 'What are the three primary memory challenges in LLM serving identified?', 'role': 'user'}, {'content': 'What throughput improvement does vLLM achieve over FasterTransformer and Orca?', 'role': 'user'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'User prompt: What are the two recovery mechanisms for preempted requests in vLLM?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention  \nAuthors: Woosuk Kwon, Zhuohan Li, Siyuan Zhuang, Ying Sheng, Lianmin Zheng, Cody Hao Yu, Joseph E. Gonzalez, Hao Zhang, Ion Stoica\n\nAbstract  \nvLLM introduces PagedAttention, a novel attention mechanism inspired by virtual memory paging, enabling efficient key-value (KV) cache management for large language model (LLM) serving. This allows for near-zero memory waste and flexible KV cache sharing. Built atop PagedAttention, vLLM achieves up to 4× throughput improvements over FasterTransformer and Orca, especially with large models and complex decoding.\n\n1. Introduction  \nLLM serving is costly and memory-bound. The main bottleneck is managing dynamic KV caches. Existing systems allocate contiguous memory, resulting in internal and external fragmentation. vLLM proposes PagedAttention to manage KV cache more like OS paging, improving batching and reducing cost.\n\n2. Background  \nCovers Transformers, KV cache role in autoregressive generation, and batching challenges. Batching must handle asynchronous arrivals and sequence length variance. Iteration-level scheduling helps reduce queueing and padding inefficiencies.\n\n3. Memory Challenges in LLM Serving  \nIdentifies three major memory issues in LLMs:  \n- Large KV cache per request  \n- Inability to share cache across outputs or sequences  \n- Fragmentation due to static, contiguous allocation  \nThese lead to inefficient GPU memory usage and limit throughput.\n\n4. Method  \n4.1 PagedAttention  \nEnables KV cache to be divided into small, fixed-size blocks (KV blocks), stored non-contiguously.\n\n4.2 KV Cache Manager  \nInspired by OS virtual memory: separates logical vs physical KV blocks using block tables, supports dynamic allocation.\n\n4.3 Decoding  \nDemonstrates how KV cache grows with decoding and how vLLM avoids pre-reservation using paged logic.\n\n4.4 Decoding Scenarios  \nSupports complex tasks like parallel sampling and beam search with memory reuse and copy-on-write. Shared prefixes are handled efficiently.\n\n4.5 Scheduling and Preemption  \nImplements FCFS scheduling and preemption strategies using block eviction. Supports both swapping (CPU RAM) and recomputation as recovery mechanisms.\n\n4.6 Distributed Execution  \nvLLM supports model parallelism (e.g., Megatron-LM) by using a centralized KV cache manager and SPMD logic.\n\n5. Implementation  \nImplemented using FastAPI and PyTorch with custom CUDA kernels. Supports various LLMs (GPT, OPT, LLaMA). Memory access is fused with attention computation for efficiency.\n\n6. Evaluation  \nvLLM achieves 2–4× throughput vs. FasterTransformer/Orca. Outperforms in basic, parallel, beam, and shared prefix decoding. Key findings:\n- Handles more batched requests\n- Memory savings up to 66% in beam search\n- Strong performance on chatbots and large models\n\n7. Ablation Studies  \n- Kernel Overhead: Small latency tradeoff (~20%) vs. major memory gains  \n- Block Size: 16 is optimal for balancing GPU use and fragmentation  \n- Recovery: Recomputation preferred for small block sizes; swapping scales with size\n\n8. Discussion  \nPagedAttention may not apply to compute-bound workloads, but is ideal for memory-bound LLM serving. Its kernel fusion techniques make the overhead manageable.\n\n9. Related Work  \nBuilds on prior systems like Orca, FasterTransformer, DeepSpeed, FlashAttention, and memory optimization techniques like FlexGen and OLLA.\n\n10. Conclusion  \nvLLM introduces paging-inspired memory management to LLM serving. PagedAttention enables non-contiguous memory allocation and KV sharing, leading to substantial throughput improvements.'}], 'rag_accuracy': 1.0, 'rag_latency': 0.004170985892415047}, {'seq_length': 59, 'latency': 0.35997010115534067, 'messages': [{'content': 'What are the three primary memory challenges in LLM serving identified?', 'role': 'user'}, {'content': 'What throughput improvement does vLLM achieve over FasterTransformer and Orca?', 'role': 'user'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'User prompt: What are the two recovery mechanisms for preempted requests in vLLM?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention  \nAuthors: Woosuk Kwon, Zhuohan Li, Siyuan Zhuang, Ying Sheng, Lianmin Zheng, Cody Hao Yu, Joseph E. Gonzalez, Hao Zhang, Ion Stoica\n\nAbstract  \nvLLM introduces PagedAttention, a novel attention mechanism inspired by virtual memory paging, enabling efficient key-value (KV) cache management for large language model (LLM) serving. This allows for near-zero memory waste and flexible KV cache sharing. Built atop PagedAttention, vLLM achieves up to 4× throughput improvements over FasterTransformer and Orca, especially with large models and complex decoding.\n\n1. Introduction  \nLLM serving is costly and memory-bound. The main bottleneck is managing dynamic KV caches. Existing systems allocate contiguous memory, resulting in internal and external fragmentation. vLLM proposes PagedAttention to manage KV cache more like OS paging, improving batching and reducing cost.\n\n2. Background  \nCovers Transformers, KV cache role in autoregressive generation, and batching challenges. Batching must handle asynchronous arrivals and sequence length variance. Iteration-level scheduling helps reduce queueing and padding inefficiencies.\n\n3. Memory Challenges in LLM Serving  \nIdentifies three major memory issues in LLMs:  \n- Large KV cache per request  \n- Inability to share cache across outputs or sequences  \n- Fragmentation due to static, contiguous allocation  \nThese lead to inefficient GPU memory usage and limit throughput.\n\n4. Method  \n4.1 PagedAttention  \nEnables KV cache to be divided into small, fixed-size blocks (KV blocks), stored non-contiguously.\n\n4.2 KV Cache Manager  \nInspired by OS virtual memory: separates logical vs physical KV blocks using block tables, supports dynamic allocation.\n\n4.3 Decoding  \nDemonstrates how KV cache grows with decoding and how vLLM avoids pre-reservation using paged logic.\n\n4.4 Decoding Scenarios  \nSupports complex tasks like parallel sampling and beam search with memory reuse and copy-on-write. Shared prefixes are handled efficiently.\n\n4.5 Scheduling and Preemption  \nImplements FCFS scheduling and preemption strategies using block eviction. Supports both swapping (CPU RAM) and recomputation as recovery mechanisms.\n\n4.6 Distributed Execution  \nvLLM supports model parallelism (e.g., Megatron-LM) by using a centralized KV cache manager and SPMD logic.\n\n5. Implementation  \nImplemented using FastAPI and PyTorch with custom CUDA kernels. Supports various LLMs (GPT, OPT, LLaMA). Memory access is fused with attention computation for efficiency.\n\n6. Evaluation  \nvLLM achieves 2–4× throughput vs. FasterTransformer/Orca. Outperforms in basic, parallel, beam, and shared prefix decoding. Key findings:\n- Handles more batched requests\n- Memory savings up to 66% in beam search\n- Strong performance on chatbots and large models\n\n7. Ablation Studies  \n- Kernel Overhead: Small latency tradeoff (~20%) vs. major memory gains  \n- Block Size: 16 is optimal for balancing GPU use and fragmentation  \n- Recovery: Recomputation preferred for small block sizes; swapping scales with size\n\n8. Discussion  \nPagedAttention may not apply to compute-bound workloads, but is ideal for memory-bound LLM serving. Its kernel fusion techniques make the overhead manageable.\n\n9. Related Work  \nBuilds on prior systems like Orca, FasterTransformer, DeepSpeed, FlashAttention, and memory optimization techniques like FlexGen and OLLA.\n\n10. Conclusion  \nvLLM introduces paging-inspired memory management to LLM serving. PagedAttention enables non-contiguous memory allocation and KV sharing, leading to substantial throughput improvements.'}], 'rag_accuracy': 1.0, 'rag_latency': 0.003962147980928421}, {'seq_length': 79, 'latency': 0.36316458974033594, 'messages': [{'content': 'What are the three primary memory challenges in LLM serving identified?', 'role': 'user'}, {'content': 'What throughput improvement does vLLM achieve over FasterTransformer and Orca?', 'role': 'user'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'User prompt: What are the two recovery mechanisms for preempted requests in vLLM?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention  \nAuthors: Woosuk Kwon, Zhuohan Li, Siyuan Zhuang, Ying Sheng, Lianmin Zheng, Cody Hao Yu, Joseph E. Gonzalez, Hao Zhang, Ion Stoica\n\nAbstract  \nvLLM introduces PagedAttention, a novel attention mechanism inspired by virtual memory paging, enabling efficient key-value (KV) cache management for large language model (LLM) serving. This allows for near-zero memory waste and flexible KV cache sharing. Built atop PagedAttention, vLLM achieves up to 4× throughput improvements over FasterTransformer and Orca, especially with large models and complex decoding.\n\n1. Introduction  \nLLM serving is costly and memory-bound. The main bottleneck is managing dynamic KV caches. Existing systems allocate contiguous memory, resulting in internal and external fragmentation. vLLM proposes PagedAttention to manage KV cache more like OS paging, improving batching and reducing cost.\n\n2. Background  \nCovers Transformers, KV cache role in autoregressive generation, and batching challenges. Batching must handle asynchronous arrivals and sequence length variance. Iteration-level scheduling helps reduce queueing and padding inefficiencies.\n\n3. Memory Challenges in LLM Serving  \nIdentifies three major memory issues in LLMs:  \n- Large KV cache per request  \n- Inability to share cache across outputs or sequences  \n- Fragmentation due to static, contiguous allocation  \nThese lead to inefficient GPU memory usage and limit throughput.\n\n4. Method  \n4.1 PagedAttention  \nEnables KV cache to be divided into small, fixed-size blocks (KV blocks), stored non-contiguously.\n\n4.2 KV Cache Manager  \nInspired by OS virtual memory: separates logical vs physical KV blocks using block tables, supports dynamic allocation.\n\n4.3 Decoding  \nDemonstrates how KV cache grows with decoding and how vLLM avoids pre-reservation using paged logic.\n\n4.4 Decoding Scenarios  \nSupports complex tasks like parallel sampling and beam search with memory reuse and copy-on-write. Shared prefixes are handled efficiently.\n\n4.5 Scheduling and Preemption  \nImplements FCFS scheduling and preemption strategies using block eviction. Supports both swapping (CPU RAM) and recomputation as recovery mechanisms.\n\n4.6 Distributed Execution  \nvLLM supports model parallelism (e.g., Megatron-LM) by using a centralized KV cache manager and SPMD logic.\n\n5. Implementation  \nImplemented using FastAPI and PyTorch with custom CUDA kernels. Supports various LLMs (GPT, OPT, LLaMA). Memory access is fused with attention computation for efficiency.\n\n6. Evaluation  \nvLLM achieves 2–4× throughput vs. FasterTransformer/Orca. Outperforms in basic, parallel, beam, and shared prefix decoding. Key findings:\n- Handles more batched requests\n- Memory savings up to 66% in beam search\n- Strong performance on chatbots and large models\n\n7. Ablation Studies  \n- Kernel Overhead: Small latency tradeoff (~20%) vs. major memory gains  \n- Block Size: 16 is optimal for balancing GPU use and fragmentation  \n- Recovery: Recomputation preferred for small block sizes; swapping scales with size\n\n8. Discussion  \nPagedAttention may not apply to compute-bound workloads, but is ideal for memory-bound LLM serving. Its kernel fusion techniques make the overhead manageable.\n\n9. Related Work  \nBuilds on prior systems like Orca, FasterTransformer, DeepSpeed, FlashAttention, and memory optimization techniques like FlexGen and OLLA.\n\n10. Conclusion  \nvLLM introduces paging-inspired memory management to LLM serving. PagedAttention enables non-contiguous memory allocation and KV sharing, leading to substantial throughput improvements.'}], 'rag_accuracy': 1.0, 'rag_latency': 0.003981353715062141}]
